-- ringserv.pack -- RingServ, the first real server placed on a machine (SRV-2).
--
-- RingServ 0.9 (Ring 1.27, vendored SQLite and QuickJS, one static binary) is
-- the application server of the Softanza estate and Haro's predecessor in that
-- seat. It serves HTTP on the port its application declares, binds the loopback
-- unless told otherwise (a front on another link is rung 3 of doc/CLOUD.md),
-- answers GET /health, and writes no file that says so. So the world is the
-- program that says it for it (`harb ready`, src/ready.zig), and the server is
-- its child: when RingServ writes its own ready file the SERVICE changes one line.
--
-- It runs as an identity of its own and not as the machine (OWN-1): the USER below,
-- and the two directories PID 1 makes and hands to it before it starts, which are
-- the places the machine gives it to write. Measured outside the machine first
-- (experiment/ringserv_nonroot.sh, which also convicts the same server run as root):
-- as an unprivileged user, from /, with an empty environment, it serves /health and
-- keeps its database in its directory. That host is not the machine's image, so this
-- shows the server and not the confinement; the boot shows the handover, from the
-- disk it leaves (experiment/os9_cloud.sh).
--
-- What it asks, and where each need was read (RingServ at origin/main 002ba21):
--   the network   a listening socket on 127.0.0.1:8210 (the application's :port)
--   the filesystem and the mount `data`   its SQLite database, which the
--                 application names `ringserv.db` and `--data` puts in /data/ringserv
--   a place of its own   /data/ringserv for what it keeps, /run/ringserv for the
--                 word that it serves
--   threads       a worker per CPU up to four, here two, and the HTTP threads: 38 threads
--                 measured at four workers, which is why TASKS is not 16
--   a clock       it timestamps what it keeps
--   process       the readiness program starts it
-- and what it does not ask: no environment (the machine gives a world none), no
-- dynamic loading (it is built without), no gpio, and no write to /tmp, which a
-- world that is not root does not have.

DEFINE USER appserver AS (
  UID 2000
) RATIONALE "The identity the application server runs as: it owns its own directories and nothing else of the machine"

DEFINE SERVICE ringserv AS (
  RUN [
    "/harb", "ready", "/run/ringserv/ready", "127.0.0.1:8210",
    "--every", "2", "--",
    "/ringserv", "run", "/app/app.ring", "--data", "/data/ringserv"
  ],
  RESTART always,
  READY "/run/ringserv/ready",
  HEALTH 10,
  NEEDS [network, filesystem, process, threads, clock],
  SEES [data],
  USER appserver,
  STATE ["/run/ringserv", "/data/ringserv"],
  MEMORY 128,
  CPU 100,
  TASKS 64
) RATIONALE "The application server, as an identity of its own: it serves once /health answers and says so by the path PID 1 waits for, every 2 seconds for as long as it keeps answering; past 128 MiB it is stopped alone, past one core it waits, past 64 tasks it is refused more"
